\begin{proof}
For each $\varepsilon>0$, there is a finite $\varepsilon$-net in $X$. Indeed, if not, recursively choose $x_1\in X$ and, after choosing $x_1,\ldots,x_k$, choose $x_{k+1}$ outside $\bigcup_{i=1}^kB_\varepsilon(x_i)$. Then $d(x_i,x_j)\geq\varepsilon$ for $i\neq j$. The resulting infinite set has no limit point, since every ball of radius $\varepsilon/2$ contains at most one of its points, contradicting the assumption on $X$. Therefore $X$ is totally bounded.

For each $n\geq1$, choose a finite $1/n$-net $F_n\subseteq X$, and set $D=\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}F_n$. Since each $F_n$ is finite, $D$ is countable. If $x\in X$ and $\delta>0$, choose $n$ with $1/n<\delta$. Because $F_n$ is a $1/n$-net, some $y\in F_n$ satisfies $d(x,y)<1/n<\delta$. Thus every neighborhood of every point of $X$ meets $D$, so $D$ is dense. Hence $X$ is separable.
\end{proof}
